In today's fast-changing world of computer technology, optimizing personal computer 
(PC) components has become crucial for people looking to enhance performance across various 
applications. This thesis explores PC component optimization, examining the methods, 
techniques, and real-world impacts of fine-tuning hardware for peak efficiency. Over the years, 
personal computers (PCs) have gone from basic devices to powerful machines that can handle 
everything from gaming to professional content creation. With software becoming more 
demanding, hardware must keep up, and that’s where PC optimization comes in. Helping users 
squeeze out more performance from their systems, sometimes even pushing beyond what 
manufacturers recommend. Optimizing a PC involves a range of strategies, from choosing the 
right parts to configuring them the right way. The goal is to find a balance between key 
components like the central processing unit (CPU), graphics processing unit (GPU), random 
access memory (RAM), and storage—because how these pieces work together is essential for 
getting the best performance and experience. 
Getting optimal performance out of your PC isn’t just an idea—it has real-world benefits 
for everyone, from pros to casual users. For example, we can look at gaming, small boosts in 
system responsiveness can be the difference between winning and losing. For professionals, 
optimizing your PC can cut down on rendering times for 3D artists or speed up compilation for 
developers, which can immensely improve productivity. The problem here is that many people 
don’t know the best ways to optimize their systems. With technology changing so quickly, 
there’s always something new to think about. The relationships between components can be 
tricky, making it hard to predict what any one tweak will do to the whole system. A lot of users 
get overwhelmed by the endless options for part selection and configuration, which can lead to 
poor performance, wasted resources, and sometimes even damage if the optimization isn’t done 
4 
right. Right now, there’s no solid, research-backed guide for how to optimize your PC across 
different use cases, and that’s a big gap in both computer science and tech for consumers. This 
gap is significant because it leaves users, from casual gamers to professional content creators, 
without reliable strategies for maximizing their PC’s performance while avoiding risks like 
instability or reduced component lifespan. A review of existing literature, as discussed in Chapter 
2, reveals scattered research focused on individual components or techniques but no 
comprehensive approach that addresses a variety of real-world use cases. This thesis aims to 
bridge that gap. 
This thesis tackles these challenges by taking a deep dive into PC component 
optimization. The main goals here are to review existing research on hardware, optimization 
strategies, and benchmarking methods; to build a solid approach for testing how different 
optimization techniques impact overall system performance; to break down specific optimization 
techniques for important parts like the CPU, GPU, RAM, and storage; to look at real-world gains 
in gaming, content creation, and everyday productivity; to offer practical recommendations for 
optimizing your PC based on different user needs; and to examine the risks that come with 
pushing optimization too far, giving advice on how to balance better performance with system 
stability.  
The structure of this thesis presents a logical progression through the topic of PC 
component optimization. Following this introduction, Chapter 2 reviews the existing research in 
the field of PC optimization. Chapter 3 outlines the research methodology, including criteria for 
selecting components and the benchmarking methods used to gather data on optimization 
techniques. Chapter 4 covers specific optimization strategies for various PC components, 
